    "id": "q01",
    "topic": "particles-wa-ga",
    "question": "What is the difference between は and が in Japanese?",
    "answer": "は marks the **topic** of the sentence — what the sentence is about. It is not always the subject. が marks the **subject** or the focus of the sentence, especially when emphasizing who or what is doing something.  \nJapanese (romaji) — translation  \nWatashi wa Kimura Rihi desu — I am Kimura Rihi (introducing the topic \"I\").  \nWatashi ga Kimura Rihi desu — I am Kimura Rihi (emphasizing that \"I\" am the subject).  \nSummary: は introduces the topic, while が highlights the subject or focus.",
