    "id": "q04",
    "topic": "particles-ni-de",
    "question": "What is the difference between に and で when talking about location?",
    "answer": "The particles **に** and **で** both relate to location but have different roles:  \n- **に** marks **destination** or **direction** (where something is going).  \n- **で** marks the **location where an action happens** (the setting of the action).  \nExamples:  \n1. 東京**に**行きます (Tokyo **ni** ikimasu) — I go **to** Tokyo (direction/destination).  \n2. 東京**で**買い物します (Tokyo **de** kaimono shimasu) — I shop **in** Tokyo (location of the action).  \n3. カフェ**で**コーヒーを飲みます (Kafe **de** koohii **wo** nomimasu) — I drink coffee **at** a café (where the action occurs).  \n**Summary:** Use **に** for *going to* a place, and **で** for *doing something* at a place.",
