  {
    "id": "q09",
    "topic": "na-adjectives",
    "question": "How do na-adjectives work in Japanese? How do you use them before a noun and in a sentence ending?",
    "answer": "Na-adjectives in Japanese are used differently from i-adjectives. Here's how they work:\n**Before a noun:**\n- Add **な** between the na-adjective and the noun.  \n  Example:  \n  きれい**な**花 (kirei **na** hana) — \"beautiful flower\"  \n  げんき**な**ひと (genki **na** hito) — \"energetic person\"\n**In sentence endings:**\n- Use **です/ではありません** (desu/janai) for statements and negatives.  \n  あの花はきれい**です** (ano hana wa kirei **desu**) — \"That flower is beautiful.\"  \n  あの花はきれい**ではありません** (ano hana wa kirei **janai**) — \"That flower is not beautiful.\"\n**Key note:**  \nNa-adjectives **do not change their own form** like i-adjectives (e.g., あつい → あつかった). Instead, they rely on **です/ではありません/でした** for tense and politeness.  \n- あの場所は静か**です** (shizuka **desu**) — \"That place is quiet.\"  \n- あの場所は静か**でした** (shizuka **deshita**) — \"That place was quiet.\"  \nNa-adjectives like きれい (kirei) and げんき (genki) are **noun-like** in behavior, requiring **な** before nouns and **です/ではありません** for sentence endings.",
