    "question": "What is the difference between ある and いる?",
    "answer": "The difference between **ある (aru)** and **いる (iru)** is based on whether the subject is **living** or **non-living**.  \n- **いる** is used for **living things** (people, animals, pets).  \n- **ある** is used for **non-living things** (objects, places, abstract concepts).  \nJapanese (romaji) — translation  \nWatashi no neko ga ie ni imasu — My cat is in the house (living thing + いる).  \nWatashi no ie ga kyoufu desu — My house is clean (non-living thing + ある).  \nKoko ni gakko ga arimasu — There is a school here (non-living thing + ある).  \nSummary: **いる = living things; ある = non-living things.**",
